## INTRODUCTION
Over the past decade, organic–inorganic metal halide based perovskites have forged ahead as the front runner among emerging photovoltaic (PV) technologies showing an amazing rise in power conversion efficiency (PCE) from merely $3.8\%$ to a certified $25.2\%$ .1-8 This approaches or even overtakes that of the rival photovoltaic technologies based on silicon, cadmium telluride (CdTe) and copper indium gallium (di) selenide (CIGS) as light absorbing materials.9 Despite the swiftly surging efficiency, the PSCs have also attracted much attention in the research community due to their unique and exceptional photoelectric properties, 10-12 facile processability and potential scalability implicating economic feasibility. What is even more exciting is that perovskite/Si tandem devices outperform crystalline Si photovoltaics showing a milestone $28\%$ PCE, representing a giant step forward towards the ultimate goal of realizing power generation from commercial perovskite based solar cells.8
Nevertheless, there are hurdles to overcome on the path to commercial success, for instance, the intrinsic thermal instability stemming from the ionic nature of the perovskite material itself and the interstitial degradation originating from unwanted interface reactions.13-15 In this regard, enhancing the lifespan of PSCs has become a main focus of the perovskite PV community. To this end, numerous attempts have been portrayed to mitigate the stability issues in terms of crystal structure effects, charge selective contact layers, electrode materials and device encapsulation.16-22
In particular, the demonstrated doping induced degradation in both the perovskite materials itself and the sandwiching charge transporting layers appears to be one of the predominant concerns for the commercialization of PSCs.7 HTMs, as one of the essential building blocks in the PSC device architecture, are responsible for hole extraction, transport of the charges from the perovskite layer to the electrode and protection of the perovskite layer from direct contact with the ambient atmosphere (viz. moisture). Therefore, the HTMs play an imperative role in both promoting high performance and enhancing PSC device longevity. Currently, the state-of-the-art PSCs are predominantly constructed employing the benchmark HTMs $^{2,2^{\circ},7,7^{\circ}}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di-p- methoxyphenylamine)-9,9’- spirobifuorene (spiro-OMeTAD), or poly(triarylamine) (PTAA).4, 6, 23 However, regardless of the exorbitant cost and tedious synthetic routes, both spiro-OMeTAD and PTAA suffer from detrimental flaws, such as intrinsically high resistivity and low charge mobility. Therefore, exploiting dopants to boost their charge transport properties has become inevitable.24-27 However, the commonly utilized dopants, such as lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI), cobalt complexes and tert-butyl pyridine (TBP) are intrinsically either hygroscopic or volatile,28-29 which leads to accelerated device degradation. Moreover, severe morphology defects, such as pinholes, are usually observed due to the migration and redistribution of the non-covalently bonded dopants.30-32 These negative consequences make the doping approach inappropriate for scalable production. In order to mitigate the negative effects on stability and performance, tremendous endeavors have been devoted to identify alternative HTMs offering more straightforward synthetic routes and good device performance in the absence of dopants. Several dopant free HTMs, including small organic molecules, metal complexes, conjugated polymers and inorganic compounds, have been explored and applied in PSCs.33-35 In spite of this, top level performance for PSCs based on dopant free HTMs has been rarely reported.
Conjugated polymers, characterized by versatile and adjustable chemical and physical properties, have been widely explored in molecular electronics in general due to their excellent carrier mobility, material stability, solution operability, film forming properties and promising compatibility with scalable roll-to-roll printing technologies in comparison with competing systems based on small organic molecules and inorganic materials.36-38 In addition, polymer based photovoltaics shows a number of merits, such as low weight, mechanical flexibility, and durability.39-40 Moreover, enhanced PSC device stability, as well as higher PCEs, have been demonstrated by the incorporation of conjugated dopants free polymers, such as RCP,41 pBBTaBDT2,42 asy-PBTBDT,43 DTP44 etc.45-48 Most of the polymeric materials used involve sophisticated molecular structures of multiple polymerized units, accordingly, jeopardizing their future practical applications from either cost or reproducibility perspectives. These concerns motivate a more facile and rational design of novel polymeric materials that can offer both low cost of production and excellent optoelectronic properties compatible with scalable production of PSCs.
In this study, three polymers, P1, P2 and P3, all based on a facile synthetic methodology have been systematically engineered and explored as dopant free HTMs and been applied in PSCs. Taking advantage of the relatively low resonance energy of thiophene building blocks, as compared with benzene, P2 and P3 promote a better charge distribution and thus facilitate charge transfer from the perovskite material to the charge collecting electrodes. The fluorination in P3 promotes a stronger inter- and intramolecular interaction due to non-covalent secondary interactions $({\mathrm{F}}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}{\mathrm{H}},{\mathrm{F}}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}{\mathrm{S}},{\mathrm{F}}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi$ , etc.), 49-51 imparting it with a closer faceon molecular packing leading to better charge mobility and thus conductivity. When applied in the PSC devices, an exceptional stabilized PCE of $20.3\%$ was attained, which is comparable to reference devices based on doped spiroOMeTAD as HTM. In addition, flexible PSCs based on P3 demonstrated an efficiency of $16.2\%$ . Moreover, device based on these new polymers demonstrate drastically improved stability.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The molecular structures of the three polymers studied in this work are depicted in Figure 1, and the detailed synthesis procedures are shown in Scheme S1. The full synthesis and purification steps are described in the Supporting Information (SI). In previous reports, the polymers made and used have complicated structures and are constructed by tedious synthetic routines.41-43, 48 All the present new polymers can facilely be constructed based on only two segments via a straightforward one step Stille coupling reaction from commercially available reagents, such as 1,4-dibromobenzene, 2,5-dibromo-thiophene and BDT ((4,8-bis(5-(2- ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene2,6-diyl)bis(trimethylstannane)).
Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) was employed to determine the molecular weight distribution, and the number molecular weights $(M_{\mathfrak{n}})$ were determined to be 1.57, 1.85 and $1.78\mathrm{{kDa}}$ with polydispersity indexes (PI) of 2.6, 2.3 and 1.9 with respect to P1, P2 and P3. The polymer P1 unexpectedly exhibits lower solubility than P2 and P3 in commonly used solvents, such as chloroform, chlorobenzene and $\scriptstyle o-$ dichlorobenzene. The normalized solution UV–Vis (ultraviolet–visible) absorption spectra and photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the polymers in chlorobenzene solvent are shown in Figure 2a and Figure S1, respectively. The corresponding film absorption is collected in Figure 2b. The light absorption of P2 and P3 are bathochromically shifted nearly $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ as compared to P1, which is expected to arise from the more planar structure and more extended conjugated $\pi$ system originating from the thiophene unit employed. P3 demonstrated a slight redshift of $3\ \mathrm{\nm}$ in absorption compared to P2. Interestingly, incorporation of fluorine in P3 causes an enhanced shoulder absorption peak at $572~\mathrm{~nm}$ when applied as film in comparison to the absorption in solution. As indicated from literature, 52 this may be attributed to polymer aggregation induced by interchain coupling $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking) between adjacent polymers in the solid film, suggesting that the fluorine incorporation may inflict closer packing of the P3 chains and thus a better charge transfer between the polymeric strands could be expected. The optical bandgap energies of the three polymers were determined to be 2.27, 2.03 and $1.98\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , respectively. In addition, the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy levels estimated from the oxidation potential of the polymer films using cyclic voltammetry are $-5.16,-5.27$ and $-5.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ for P1, P2, and P3, respectively. (Figure S2). All results are in good accordance with those from ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS; Figure 2c). The lowest unoccupied orbital (LUMO) energy levels were resolved to $-2.89$ , $-3.24$ and $-3.40\ \mathrm{eV}$ based on the respective bandgaps. The frontier orbitals of the polymers match those of the perovskite material well (Figure 2d). This allows simultaneous efficient extraction of holes from the perovskite and blocking of shunt pathways from the perovskite to the Au back contact. Furthermore, thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) were exploited to monitor thermal and phase stability of the polymers. As revealed in Figure 2e and Figure 2f, the decomposition temperatures corresponding to $5\%$ weight loss were determined to 378, 405 and $420~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, and no apparent peak could be observed in the DSC curves, indicating that the polymers are sufficiently thermally stable with no detectable phase transition up to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , conferring the polymers with traits of low crystallinity and high thermal stability in the film state. All the optical, thermal and electrochemical properties of the polymers are collected in Table S1.
In order to further investigate the structure-properties relationship of the polymers, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed. The optimized backbone geometries, as well as the frontier orbitals are depicted in Figure 1 and Figure S3, respectively. The dihedral angles between the adjacent BDT and benzene or thiophene units were determined to $22.4^{\circ}$ , $0.3^{\circ}$ and $0.3^{\circ}$ in P1, P2 and P3, respectively. Consequently, P2 and P3 containing a bridging thiophene unit exhibit larger backbone planarity as compared to the analogous P1, highlighting the beneficial role of the thiophene unit with respect to molecular geometry promoting efficient $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking to facilitate charge transport. In order to further illustrate the effects generated by the $\mathbf{P3}~\mathrm{F}$ atoms, calculations on polymer strands were carried out and the corresponding data are depicted in Figure S4. From the simulated results, P3 was noted to favor an ordered stacking configuration, while P2 shows a disordered structure, indicating that the fluorine incorporation induces enhanced interaction between the polymer chains.
The charge transporting capability of the polymeric materials was evaluated utilizing the space charge limited current method (SCLC) (Figure 3a and Table 1). The hole mobility $(\mu_{\mathrm{h}})$ of $\mathbf{P}2$ and P3 was estimated to $3.8\times10^{-4}$ and $5.7{\times}10^{-4}\:\mathrm{cm^{2}\cdot V^{-1}\cdot S^{-1}}$ , which is one order of magnitude higher than that of P1 $(3.0{\times}10^{-5}\thinspace\mathrm{cm^{2}\cdot V^{-1}\cdot s^{-1}})$ and the commonly used spiro-OMeTAD $(7.7{\times}10^{-5}\ \mathrm{cm^{2.}V^{-1}{\cdot}s^{-1}})$ . These experimental results are in good agreement with those from the DFT calculations, in which the planar conformation of P2 and P3 implicate a favorable $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking to form charge transporting channels. The lower hole mobility of P1 can probably be attributed to the higher resonance energy in the bridging benzene ring rendering a more twisted polymeric structure hampering intra- and inter-molecular interactions. The stronger interaction between the connecting polymer strands in P3 promoted by non-covalent interactions is expected to form closer molecular stacking, and as a consequence, contribute to the excellent $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ .52 In addition, the conductivity of the polymers was also examined (Figure S5). The trend in conductivity further validates the more efficient charge delocalization for the polymers incorporating a thiophene unit instead of a benzene unit.
In order to get an insight into the polymer packing and the relationship between molecular orientation and the charge transporting ability, grazing-incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) was performed (Figure 3b and Figure 3c).53-54 As indicated above, P2 and P3 demonstrated distinct lamellar stacking in contrast to P1. The neat P1 film shows a typical edge-on orientation with $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking alongside the in-plane direction $(q_{\mathrm{z}}\approx0.29\textrm{\AA}^{-1})$ ), whilst P2 and P3 both exhibit an evident face-on structure with clear $\pi{-}\pi$ lamellar stacking peak (010) in the out-of-plane direction $(q_{\mathrm{v}}\approx1.7\mathrm{\AA}^{-}$ 1), which is consistent with the inferior vertical hole mobility of P1 with respect to P2 and P3. The face-on orientation is considered to facilitate the charge transport in a vertical diode configuration of the solar cells.55 Moreover, according to Bragg’s law, P3 shows a shorter $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of $(3.62\pm0.02)$ Å as compared to P2 $(3.84\pm0.01)\mathrm{\AA}$ , further validating the expected role of fluorine in favoring a more close stacking of the polymer chains by non-covalent interactions.56 This result also offers an explanation of the superior hole mobility of P3 due to the better orbital overlap between the neighboring polymer strands. To summarize, the GIWAXS results are in good agreement with the film absorption spectra and the mobility investigations. The preferential face-on orientations, as well as the tighter polymer chains stacking in P3, may contribute to the better charge-transport properties and thus the better performance of the resulting PSCs devices.
Field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) were carried out to investigate the film morphology and formation properties of the polymers on top of perovskite films. As disclosed in the SEM images in Figure S6 (a–d), compared with the pristine perovskite films, P2 and P3 were observed to generate uniform films well covering the underlying perovskite layer. In contrast, P1 shows many rugged and island-like aggregates. This is presumably due to the relatively low solubility of P1. AFM images were used to investigate the nanoscale morphology, as shown in Figure S6 (e-h). The root-meansquare (RMS) roughness was determined to ${\sim}10.2$ , 8.5, 5.1 and $4.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ , for the neat perovskite film, P1/perovskite, P2/perovskite and P3/perovskite, respectively. The significantly lowered surface roughness of the P2 and P3 films indicate an efficient coverage of the perovskite film. The rougher surface observed for the P1 film should be ascribed to the low solubility of P1 in the precursor solution as noted above. Overall, the favorable morphology of P2 and P3 films is anticipated to block undesired shunt loss transfers caused by the direct contact of the perovskite and counter electrode.
In order to demonstrate the potential of the polymers as HTMs in PSCs, devices with the regular configuration Glass/(flourine doped tin oxide) FTO/mesoporous $(m p)-$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /Perovskite/HTM/Au were fabricated. A uniform $\mathrm{[CH_{3}N H_{3}]P b I_{3}}$ $({\mathrm{MAPbI}}_{3}$ ) light absorbing layer was deposited via a solvent bathing approach.57 Details of the PSC fabrication can be found in the SI. All polymers were applied in their pristine form without any dopants. Firstly, optimization of the film thickness of the polymers was performed by modulating the concentrations of the polymeric precursors from $3\mathrm{mg/mL}$ to $15\mathrm{mg/mL}$ in chlorobenzene, and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are collected in Figure S7, Figure S8, Figure S9 and Table S2, Table S3, Table S4, respectively. The optimal thickness is achieved from a low precursor concentration of merely ${5\mathrm{mg/mL}}$ , being significantly lower than the ${>}80\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ required for spiro
OMeTAD. In a future production prospect, this would indicate a low materials consumption and thus low cost (Figure S10, Figure S11, Table S5, Table S6, Table S7 and Table S8). Ca. $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick HTM layers were obtained uniformly capping the surface of a ${\sim}450~\mathrm{nm}$ thick perovskite layer as observed in the cross-sectional view of the device (inset of Figure 4a). The performance becomes lower as the concentration is increased from 5 to $15~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , in particular lower the $F F$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Most likely a thicker film experiences charge transport limitations because of the intrinsically low conductivity of organic materials. Low polymer precursor concentrations $(3\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) resulted in incomplete coverage of the perovskite film, leading to severe shunt losses at the interface. The optimized photovoltaic performance is collected in Figure 4a and Table 1. P1 based PSCs exhibit an inferior PCE of mere $14.9\%$ (reverse scan) with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of
$21.8~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.01\mathrm{V}$ and a $F F$ of 0.68, accompanied with serious hysteresis effect (Figure S12). This can most likely be linked to the polymer film properties of P1 discussed above. In contrast, the P2 and P3 demonstrated excellent performance in the PSCs. P2 based devices offered a champion PCE of $19.6\%$ (reverse scan) with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of 22.8 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.08{\mathrm{V}}$ and a $F F$ of 0.79 along with a small hysteresis effect (Figure 4b). P3 gave devices with a remarkable PCE of $20.3\%$ and with negligible hysteresis (Figure 4c), with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.11~\mathrm{~V~}$ and $F F$ of 0.80, representing as one of the best PCEs reported employing dopant free polymeric HTMs. These results are essentially the same as recorded for devices based on the reference material doped spiro-OMeTAD (Figure S13). All results are in good agreement with the GIWAXS results.
Incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) was recorded to elucidate the origin of the device $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ , as depicted in Figure 4d. Devices based on P2 and P3 both demonstrate highly similar photon-to-current efficiency in the whole spectral range from $300\mathrm{nm}$ to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ comparable to that of the reference spiro-OMeTAD, The IPCE for devices based on P1 was as expected much lower. Integrating the IPCE curves resulted in an integral $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of 20.1, 22.3, 22.4 and $22.3\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , with respect to devices based on P1, P2, P3 and reference spiro-OMeTAD. These results are consistent with the currents derived from the $J{-}V$ measurements. The statistical analysis of 20 devices verified a good reproducibility of the performance for devices based on P2 and P3, while those based on P1 showed a pronounced PCE variation distributed between $8\%$ and $14\%$ (Figure S14). In order to detect the reproducibility of the polymer synthesis and product purity, four different batches of P3 were synthesized and applied in solar cells. Statistic data shown in Figure S15 imply good reproducibility of P3 both with respect to synthesis and device performance. The power output at the maximum power point (MPP) was tracked with time, and stable PCEs of $19.4\%$ , $20.3\%$ were observed for over 1500 s for devices based on P2 and P3, respectively (Figure 4e). In order to explore the influence of additives on the PSC performances, the same doping substances and levels as used for the spiro-OMeTAD HTM were added to the polymeric HTMs. Unfortunately, the dopants lower the solubility of the polymer precursors and hence the film formation. As the results shown in Figure S16 and Table S9, all the doped polymers exhibit much lower performance relative to their non-doped forms.
Furthermore, in order to investigate the potential of the polymers for scalable application in flexible devices, PSCs were fabricated on polyethylene terephthalate (PET) substrate with indium doped tin(IV) oxide (ITO) as conductive transparent layer with a device architecture $\mathrm{PET/ITO/}m p\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SnO_{2}/M A P b I_{3}/H T M/A u}$ employing P3 as HTM (Figure 4f). A champion efficiency of $16.2\%$ was recorded, manifesting the promising prospective of facile roll-to-roll production.
In order to investigate the interfacial charge transport properties behind the excellent photovoltaic performance, steady state PL quenching (Figure $\mathbf{4g}$ ) and time resolved PL decay (TRPL) (Figure 4h) were explored. Capping the perovskite layer with a film of all the polymers, the PL intensities were quenched to different extent, indicating that the polymers are able to extract holes from the perovskite layer. And as anticipated, the P2 and P3 layers work the best. Normalization of the PL curves show a slight blueshift and narrowing full width at half maximum (FWHM) in the P2 and P3 based systems (Figure S17). This indicates a lower trap state density in the perovskite films, and accordingly a suppressed interfacial charge recombination loss and enhanced hole collection efficiency. The TRPL decay data were modeled by a biexponential equation as illustrated in Table S10. The results are consistent with the steady state PL spectra, where the PL lifetime of the pure perovskite film is dramatically reduced after capping with the different polymers. The P3 system demonstrated the shortest PL lifetime, again attributed to its ordered polymer packing.
In order to directly study the trap state density passivation effect by the polymers, hole only devices with the structure ITO/Poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene)- poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT: PSS)/MAPbI3/HTM/Au were constructed (Figure S18). The trap filling limited voltages $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ were determined to 1.10, 1.07, 0.88 and 0.74 V, with respect to the pure perovskite film, as well as nondoped P1, P2 and P3 capped ones. The corresponding trap state densities $\left(n_{\mathrm{trap}}\right)$ were estimated using the equation of $n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ $=(2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}V_{\mathrm{TFL}})/(e L^{2})$ , where $e$ is the elementary charge, $L$ is the film thickness, $\varepsilon_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity, and $\varepsilon$ is the relative dielectric constant. The trap state densities were determined to $1.87{\times}10^{16}~\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$ , $1.72{\times}10^{16}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $1.42\times10^{16}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , and $1.18{\times}10^{16}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. The trap state densities of perovskite surfaces capped with P2 and P3 are only 2/3 of the neat perovskite films. As discussed in literature, 46 the planar conjugated backbones of polymers are electron-rich, enabling the electron donating groups, such as thiophene, to act as Lewis bases to partially passivate the trap state sites, such as un-occupied $\mathrm{\sfPb}$ atoms on the surfaces and grain boundaries of the perovskite films. In addition, the fluorine atoms was indicated to have an effect judging from the lowest $n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ determined for the P3 based system.42 The much lower trap state density is expected to offer less recombination centers and thus result in a higher photocurrent. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ decay traces were studied further for understanding and the results are given in Figure S19. The $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ (charge recombination resistance)
extracted from the Nyquist plots confirm higher resistances for P2 and P3 based devices as compared to the P1 and doped spiro-OMeTAD based ones supporting the hypothesis of inhibited interface recombination processes. Meanwhile, the dramatically prolonged $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ decays in P2 and P3 based samples perfectly match the EIS results and the trap state density analysis. Thus, the planar molecular conformation as well as the efficient polymer strand packing not only promote the hole extraction ability but also simultaneously seem to passivate the surface defects and reduce recombination losses eventually contributing to the high device $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ .
In order to evaluate the water resistance of the polymer capped perovskite films, contact angle measurements were performed (Figure S20). Larger contact angles with respect to water were perceived employing P2 and P3 as top layer as compared to the neat perovskite film or perovskite films covered with doped-spiro-OMeTAD. This implies a higher hydrophobicity of the dopant free polymers as protecting layers. Aiming to directly demonstrate the degradation processes of the perovskite films, the film stabilities under ambient conditions were tracked with time (Figure 5a), and XRD (X-ray diffraction pattern) were recorded at regular intervals to estimate the degradation of the device layers (Figure 5b-d). The devices based on doped spiro-OMeTAD showed a clear morphology degradation after only 2 weeks of storage, which could be ascribed to the hygroscopic dopants LiTFSI and FK209 expected to accelerate decomposition processes involving water. After 6 weeks, the perovskite film was practically completely decomposed to a pale yellow film, in which no XRD perovskite peaks could be detected (Figure 5b). Meanwhile, the perovskite devices covered by P2 and P3 films retained the uniformly shiny appearance even 6 weeks into the stability tests, and no apparent impurity peaks could be observed in the XRD patterns (Figure 5c and Figure 5d). This highlights the eminent effects of the dopant free polymers concerning protection of the perovskite from aggressive environmental moisture. Furthermore, the stability of the PSCs based on the different HTMs was also investigated. All devices were kept under ambient atmosphere without any encapsulation at humidities of $20\mathrm{-}70\%$ (Figure 5e). The P2 and P3 based PSCs maintained over $96\%$ of the original performance after 40 days of the stability test, whereas the reference of doped spiro-OMeTAD based PSCs suffered a declining performance retaining only $50\%$ of the initial performance after 40 days. This shows that the new polymers studied in this work not only offer high photovoltaic performance but also promote the durability of the PSC devices.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, a series of cost effective polymers constructed via a facile approach have been designed and explored as dopant free HTMs in PSCs. A planar molecular conformation was conferred with a favorable face-on orientation to give a close interaction between the polymer strands, as well as with the perovskite material, leading to the good charge transport properties, passivated trap state defects at the HTMperovskite interface and suppressed recombination losses. All these effects sum up to offer stabilized PCEs of $20.3\%$ for devices based on the HTM P3; among the best performance recorded for PSCs based on dopant-free HTMs. In addition, the polymers can be employed in flexible PSCs showing PCEs of $16.2\%$ . This shows the applicability of these polymeric and dopant-free HTMs in scalable applications. More importantly, the hydrophobic features of the polymers conferred the PSCs with traits of elongated durability and morphology stability. The excellent performance in combination with promising long-term stability for devices based on the newly designed low cost, dopant free, polymeric HTMs offer a promising foreground for the development of commercially viable PSCs.
